# Adhar AI — the agent runtime's outward surface (ADR-0024 §8, narrowed by
# ADR-0025).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS ROUTE MOVED OFF ai.<host> AND mcp.<host>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It used to claim BOTH of those hostnames for `adhar-ai-runtime`. agentgateway
# now claims exactly the same two on the same Gateway (`adhar-gateway`), because
# they are the platform's AI *protocol* endpoints and agentgateway is the thing
# that speaks those protocols:
#
#   ai.<host>/v1/chat/completions   -> agentgateway -> Anthropic / OpenAI / vLLM
#   mcp.<host>/mcp                  -> agentgateway -> the seven MCP servers,
#                                      federated, JWT-validated, authorized per
#                                      tool
#
# Two HTTPRoutes claiming one hostname on one Gateway is not a configuration —
# Gateway API resolves the overlap by creation timestamp, so which one serves a
# request depends on which Application happened to sync first. So the runtime
# gives both hostnames up.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CHOICE: ITS OWN HOSTNAME, NOT A PATH UNDER ANOTHER ONE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   agent.adhar.localtest.me  ->  adhar-ai-runtime:8080
#
# ADR-0025 offered two shapes — a path under a hostname the runtime owns (e.g.
# `adhar.<host>/ai/*`) or a hostname of its own. A hostname is chosen because:
#
#   * the runtime serves at the ROOT of its own namespace (/healthz, /config,
#     /chat, /findings, /operators/{name}/event). Hanging that off a path prefix
#     needs a URLRewrite to strip the prefix on every request, and any absolute
#     path the app ever emits then points outside the prefix. A hostname needs
#     no rewrite and no application change.
#   * `<app>.<host>` is the convention every other package follows
#     (console.<host>, vllm.<host>, keycloak.<host>) and the `*.<host>` wildcard
#     cert on adhar-gateway already covers it — no new certificate, no new DNS
#     record, no new host port.
#   * it keeps the three AI surfaces nameable and separately governable:
#     `ai.` is completions, `mcp.` is tools, `agent.` is the reasoning loop.
#
# The literal `adhar.localtest.me` is the platform's seed-time host convention:
# the controller rewrites it to the real domain (and port suffix) when the stack
# is seeded into Gitea. Never hardcode another domain.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IDENTITY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This hostname does NOT pass through agentgateway, so the runtime is the one
# AI surface that still authenticates for itself, against the Keycloak `adhar-ai`
# client (oidc-client.yaml) whose `groups` mapper the gateway's CEL rules also
# read. The MCP servers behind it no longer validate anything themselves — the
# gateway does that for the federated endpoint (ADR-0025 item 6).
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: adhar-ai
  namespace: adhar-system
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: adhar-ai
    adhar.io/component: ai
    adhar.io/plane: control
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - name: adhar-gateway
      namespace: adhar-system
  hostnames:
    - agent.adhar.localtest.me
  rules:
    # The root is a redirect, because the runtime does not serve one.
    #
    # adhar-ai-runtime is an API: /healthz, /config, /chat (POST), /findings,
    # /operators/{name}/event, and FastAPI's own /docs. There is no handler for
    # `/`, so opening https://agent.<host>/ in a browser returned a bare 404 —
    # and a named platform surface answering 404 at its root reads as "adhar-ai
    # did not come up" even when all ten of its pods are Running and its Argo CD
    # Application is Synced + Healthy. That is exactly how it was reported on the
    # AWS cluster (2026-10-09); nothing was wrong with the service.
    #
    # So `/` now sends a browser to the OpenAPI explorer the runtime already
    # serves, which proves the surface is alive and says what it offers.
    #
    # THE PORT MUST BE STATED, and this file is a .tmpl for that one reason.
    # Leaving it out does not "inherit the request's port" — Gateway API
    # defaults a redirect's port to the listener's, so Cilium emitted
    # `location: https://agent.<host>:443/docs` (measured on the AWS cluster).
    # That is right in cloud and wrong locally, where the Gateway listens on 443
    # inside the cluster but the platform is published on 8443: the browser
    # would be sent to a port nothing answers on. Exactly the Gitea
    # `/user/login` trap, which is where this is copied from.
    #
    # Exact beats PathPrefix in Gateway API's precedence rules, so this rule wins
    # for `/` and every other path still reaches the runtime below. The human
    # chat UI is the Console's /ai page; this hostname stays the machine surface.
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: Exact
            value: /
      filters:
        - type: RequestRedirect
          requestRedirect:
            path:
              type: ReplaceFullPath
              replaceFullPath: /docs
            port: {{ .Port }}
            statusCode: 302
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: /
      backendRefs:
        - name: adhar-ai-runtime
          port: 8080
